      "provenance": {
        "notes": "NAME CLASH and look-alikes: this is English lavender; L. stoechas (Spanish or French lavender, cantueso) and L. x intermedia are different plants. RHS says English lavenders are hardy and long-lived, French lavender and the tender species slightly less hardy and short-lived; the shop labels in Spain mix them. Starter list rows mix species too; this entry is L. angustifolia only. Watering: no page gives pot advice or a schedule; RHS says free-draining soil, hates wet in winter; NC State says perfectly drained, preferably on the dry side, root rot from overwatering. let_fully_dry is the agent's mapping; interval 10 days is a guess. Light: RHS sun-lover, dislikes shade; NC State full sun (partial shade also listed), stored direct. Size: RHS 30 to 90 cm tall for the species (cultivars 10 to 100), no species spread; NC State up to 2 ft tall by 3 ft wide (60 by 90 cm); height 90 is RHS's upper value, spread 90 is NC State's, hence from_knowledge_verify. Growth medium is NC State. Hardiness: RHS H5 per search summary only, not on the fetched pages; NC State USDA zones 5a to 9b; no temperature stored. Toxicity: ASPCA toxic to cats and dogs (linalool, linalyl acetate; nausea, vomiting, inappetence), no severity word; NC State tags problem for cats and dogs and rates human severity low with the same agents and signs. Stored 1 for cats, dogs and children (agent's grading). NC State says no contact dermatitis, so no skin entry. Es and it names are machine drafts, not fetched."
      },
